diff --git a/scripts/porthole/README.md b/scripts/porthole/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4c8a652
--- /dev/null
+++ b/scripts/porthole/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+# Transport regression test
+
+This harness links the production `NetLink.cpp`, ENet, and the complete
+`SaveXfer.cpp` sender and receiver. Its only engine adapter supplies an isolated
+directory containing generated bytes. No game assets or existing saves are read.
+Steam integration is linked but never initialized.
+
+Use an x64 Visual C++ / Windows SDK command prompt with `cl.exe`, `link.exe`,
+`INCLUDE` and `LIB` configured. The tested compiler is VS2010 SP1 x64
+16.00.40219.01. Python 3.9 or newer is required. From the repository root:
+
+```powershell
+python scripts/porthole/build_netlink_test.py --output <workspace>/transport-build
+python scripts/porthole/test_netlink_relay.py <new-evidence-directory> --exe <workspace>/transport-build/NetLinkQA.exe
+```
+
+Four cases run against an actual pair of production network workers:
+
+* Normal UDP without a profile, allowing 1400-byte datagrams.
+* Negative control: unmodified UDP through a 1200-byte datagram blackhole.
+  This case must fail to deliver motion and the complete save.
+* `KENSHICOOP_NETWORK_PROFILE=porthole` through the same blackhole.
+* The profile with 25 ± 15 ms one-way latency, 1% seeded datagram loss,
+  384 KiB/s per direction and a bounded relay queue.
+
+Positive cases require a generated 3 MiB save and metadata to arrive with matching
+SHA-256 per file, successful receiver commit and sender ACK, motion and reliable
+control progress in both directions, and maximum observed motion gap / control
+latency below three seconds. The production transfer also validates its own CRC.
+The output directory must not already exist. All generated files and logs stay
+there. A simulated blackhole is a regression condition, not a claim that Porthole
+itself imposes a 1200-byte limit.
+
+For an independently prepared UDP tunnel, the native harness can also run
+directly. First create `<host-test-dir>/save/generated/quick.save` with generated
+test bytes and create the empty guest test directory. Set the profile in both
+processes and run concurrently:
+
+```powershell
+$env:KENSHICOOP_NETWORK_PROFILE='porthole'
+NetLinkQA.exe host <backend-port> <host-test-dir> <host-result.json> 40000
+NetLinkQA.exe join <mapped-local-port> <guest-test-dir> <guest-result.json> 40000
+```
+
+The guest connects to `127.0.0.1:<mapped-local-port>`. Check the JSON, sender ACK,
+receiver commit, and independently compare every generated file's SHA-256. This
+tests a large reliable host-to-guest transfer and bidirectional controls/motion.
+It does not establish WAN performance, a reverse large transfer, combat or other
+gameplay correctness.
+
+The opt-in profile uses a conservative 1200-byte ENet MTU and 14-character
+unreliable batches. It submits roughly 16 KiB of the save stream at most every
+50 ms and pauses bulk submission while a peer has at least 64 KiB of bulk payload
+pending inside ENet. BEGIN/FILE/DONE retain their FIFO order; an individual
+oversized protocol packet is submitted whole. This bounds submitted bulk bursts,
+not the complete application producer queue or all possible network latency.
+Ordinary UDP keeps its existing packet sizes and save submission behavior.
